# Rail-Pay

Permissionless FX settlement on Monad. Somebody in London types `send 50k to mum` into a chat,
approves with their face, and a bank account in Lagos receives naira. Local providers compete in a
sealed-bid auction to deliver it, and whatever they do not take comes back to the sender.

Written for: judges, and anyone picking this repo up cold.

Last updated 10 October 2026. 79 commits since 16 September.

---

## 1. The problem, in money

Sending $200 to sub-Saharan Africa costs **8.46%** on average. The global average is **6.36%**.
Those are World Bank figures, Remittance Prices Worldwide Q3 2025, and they have barely moved in a
decade despite years of fintechs promising otherwise.

The cost is not in the technology. Moving a dollar between two databases is free. The cost is that
**the price is set by whoever you happened to sign up with**, and you cannot see what anybody else
would have charged. Each provider quotes a rate, hides a margin inside it, and the sender has no way
to make them compete.

Rail does not try to be a cheaper provider. **It makes providers bid against each other for the job,
in the open, on every single transfer** — and it replaces "trust this provider you have never heard
of" with collateral, escrow and settlement anyone can check.

---

## 2. How it works

Seven steps, all running on Monad testnet today.

1. **A sender escrows dollars.** One EIP-3009 `receiveWithAuthorization` signature moves AUSD into
   `RailCore`. The sender pays no gas: a relayer submits it and is reimbursed from a declared fee.
2. **An auction opens.** Every escrowed transfer is public the moment it exists.
3. **Providers bid sealed.** `commitBid(orderId, commitment)` where the commitment is
   `keccak256(orderId, lp, amount, salt)`. Nobody can see anyone else's price.
4. **They reveal.** `revealBid(orderId, amount, salt)`. The leading bid locks collateral at **110%**
   of itself. A bid whose provider has not staked enough is skipped even if it was lowest.
5. **The winner collects the account number.** Only the winner can, proved by a signature the
   relayer checks against `order.winner` on chain.
6. **They pay from their own bank.** Entirely off Rail, on their own licence, with a reference like
   `RAILA8BEFD38`. **Rail never touches naira.**
7. **Settlement.** The winner calls `markPaid`. A Chainlink CRE workflow checks the bank feed and,
   if every node agrees the money landed, an attestation releases the escrow at once; otherwise a
   dispute window runs and **anyone** can `finalize` when it closes. The winner receives exactly its
   bid and `maxAusd − bid` returns to the sender. If the provider never pays, **anyone** calls
   `refund` and its collateral is slashed to the sender.

### Measured on the live pilot

Three orders on 9–10 October, two independent provider bots bidding against each other:

| Order | Created → settled | CRE attested before the window closed by | Returned to sender |
|---|---|---|---|
| `0x8f0f289f…` | 511 blocks (~154s) | — (attested 2 blocks late; settled by the window) | $0.45 |
| `0xa9fb426c…` | 363 blocks (~110s) | 148 blocks | $0.45 |
| `0x7e8678da…` | 410 blocks (~124s) | 103 blocks | $0.45 |

About two minutes, end to end, of which ~91 seconds is the sealed auction itself. The provider bots'
bank payouts are **simulated** on the pilot; everything else in that table happened on chain.

---

## 3. The nine invariants

These are in `CLAUDE.md` and every change in this repo is checked against them.

1. **Rail never holds, moves, or touches fiat.** Providers pay from their own accounts under their
   own licences. This is the whole regulatory argument.
2. **Chat proposes; the sender's own key authorises.** The bot holds no signing key and has no code
   path to any endpoint that moves funds. A test drives every command past a stub relayer and asserts
   `/v1/orders` is never reached.
3. **No PII on-chain.** Bank details appear only as a salted `keccak256` commitment.
4. **`finalize`, `refund` and `claim` are permissionless.** *"What if your backend dies?"* →
   *"Nothing. Anyone can finalize."*
5. **The sender captures the auction saving.**
6. **A provider always has more locked than it could steal.** 110% collateral, slashed to the wronged
   sender rather than to a treasury.
7. **Reputation is derived, never written.** Counters incremented only by `RailCore`.
8. **The optimistic layer is never removed.** Attestors can only *accelerate* settlement.
9. **One signature from the sender.** Order parameters are hashed into the EIP-3009 nonce, so a
   relayer cannot alter the recipient, amount, currency or attestor.

---

## 4. What is built

| Part | Stack | Tests |
|---|---|---|
| `contracts/` | Solidity 0.8.26, Foundry, OpenZeppelin v5 | **47**, 32 adversarial |
| `services/relayer/` | TypeScript, Node type-stripping, viem | **87** |
| `services/matcher/` | TypeScript, provider bidding bot | **16** |
| `bot/` | TypeScript, Telegram + WhatsApp transports | **114** |
| `web/` | Next.js 16, React 19, Tailwind 4, Motion | e2e via Chrome + WebAuthn |
| `cre/` | Chainlink CRE workflow, TypeScript SDK | **15** |
| `indexer/` | Envio HyperIndex | signature check |

**279 automated tests.**

### Contracts

`RailCore` (escrow, auction, settlement, dispute, refund), `LPRegistry` (stake, collateral,
slashing, append-only reputation), `SignedAttestor` (layered attestations that can accelerate
settlement). Attack tests are written before implementation, each with a one-line comment stating
the attack in plain English.

### Services

The **relayer** quotes, submits, and holds recipient bank details encrypted at rest. It holds MON for
gas and nothing else. Its **sweeper** refunds any auction nobody bid on and finalizes any order whose
window has closed — those calls are permissionless, which in practice meant nobody made them until an
empty auction left a sender's $76.63 in escrow for a day. On the testnet it also tops up practice
dollars from Agora's faucet for accounts that hold no MON.

The **matcher** is the provider bot: it watches for orders over a WebSocket, prices them, bids,
reveals and confirms. Two run on Railway with different margins; on the pilot their payouts are
simulated and recorded where the CRE workflow can check them.

### The chat bot

People write "can you send 20k to my mum please" and "is this safe?", not commands, so the bot
understands everyday phrasing and answers questions about Rail in full sentences. It stays a payments
bot, never a general assistant (banned on the WhatsApp Business Platform since January 2026):
off-topic requests get a polite redirect, and anything that moves money still becomes a link the
sender approves with Face ID. An optional Claude layer widens what it can follow; it cannot act,
never sees a run of 8+ digits, and a reply using crypto vocabulary is discarded.

Providers say `alerts` and every new request arrives in their chat within seconds, with the amount,
the sender's ceiling, the time left and a link to bid.

---

## 5. Sponsor technology, and what it actually does here

### Monad

Not a deployment target, a **requirement**. A sealed-bid auction needs two rounds, because on a
transparent ledger a bid is public the moment it lands. Two rounds inside a payment is only
affordable where blocks are **302ms** (measured over 1000 blocks) and fees are near zero. On a chain
with twelve-second blocks and dollar gas, a sealed auction on a $38 transfer is absurd.

### Agora — AUSD

The settlement asset, chosen for **native EIP-3009**: a sender who has never touched crypto signs
one authorisation and pays no gas. The EIP-712 domain is read from the asset at signing time rather
than hardcoded. Agora's test faucet funds practice accounts in one tap.

### Mera — passkeys

The account layer for somebody who has never held crypto. `createPasskeyWithPrfOutput` → PRF output →
BIP-39 → HDKey `m/44'/60'/0'/0/{index}` → a viem account that signs EIP-712. **No private key is ever
stored**; the signing session is ended immediately in every path including failure.

**One passkey, many keys:** index 0 is the spending account and index 1 a savings account, derived
from the *same* PRF output with no second passkey and no second prompt. An e2e test with a Chrome
virtual authenticator asserts the derivation is deterministic.

### Dynamic — provider sign-in

Providers sign in on `/provider` through Dynamic: a browser wallet, a phone wallet over
WalletConnect, or just an email, which creates a wallet the provider holds. Every protocol call on
the page takes the viem `WalletClient` Dynamic returns, switched to Monad testnet before each write.
Dynamic loads on the provider route only, so senders never download it and their passkey path is
unchanged.

### Chainlink CRE — the payout attestor

The workflow (`cre/payout-attestor`) is an HTTP-triggered DON job: every node queries the bank feed
for the order's reference and the DON attests only on **identical** agreement — the strictest
consensus, affordable because of invariant 8 (disagreement means "slower", never "stuck"). A node
that cannot reach the bank throws rather than voting "not paid".

It runs in the CRE simulator: deployment needs Early Access, and CRE has no Monad forwarder yet. A
relay (`relay.ts`) watches for paid orders, runs the compiled workflow against the winning provider's
feed, and only on `attested: true` signs a layer-3 attestation with a key `SignedAttestor`
authorises, then finalizes. That key is the remaining trust point until a forwarder exists; it can
only make settlement sooner, never cause a refund or a slash.

### Envio — HyperIndex

One indexer over all four deployments, and it is load-bearing in four places: the sender dashboard
reads history by sender (so the same account shows the same transfers on every device), the provider
page reads open requests and wins, the relayer's sweeper finds stranded orders, and the bot's
provider alerts come from it. `scripts/check.mjs` verifies every event signature against the
deployed ABIs.

---

## 6. Decisions worth defending

**Why the sender never sees crypto vocabulary.** A grep over the sender UI and every bot string must
return zero hits for `wallet`, `gas`, `crypto`, `token`, `AUSD`, `Monad` and twenty more. Partly
WhatsApp's commerce policy; mostly because somebody sending money to their mother is not shopping for
a financial product.

**Why 150-block windows, when 30 was measured as enough.** Thirty blocks is twelve seconds:
comfortable for a bot, impossible for a person. A window only software can reach makes this a
professionals-only market, which is the thing Rail exists to replace.

**Why there is nothing to deposit.** A prepaid balance would have to be spendable by something. If
the bot can spend it, invariant 2 is gone. So the money stays in the sender's own account until the
authorisation pulls exactly what the transfer costs.

**Why nobody can bid for a provider.** The contract checks that a bid comes from the key that
staked, which is what makes the collateral mean anything. Autopilot on the provider page signs with
the provider's own wallet; a hands-free bot holds its own dedicated key.

